#!/bin/bash

# Shamelessly stolen from https://github.com/mintsuki/dir2fat32-esp
#
# The file copy feature to seed the partition was removed.
#
# It now simply replaces the output if it exists.  No 'force' option
# is needed.  No option parsing.  Just print usage if run without
# arguments.
#
# The play-by-play output replaced with a single line showing what was
# done.  Since it's run with -e it will never get there if it fails.
#
# Uses mkfs.vfat instead of mformat.
#
# Note: while the disk image is FAT32, the partition created is
# actually of the type EFI System.  Often the type is either this or
# MS Basic Data, and probing is needed to identify it as FAT32.  This
# is an important test case as it exercises probing functionality.
#

# Create a FAT32 disk image from the contents of a directory.
#
# This tool requires the following to be available on the host system:
#
# - util-linux
# - mtools
#
# Copyright 2016 Othernet Inc, 2021 mintsuki
